    period: "Arthurian Era",
    aliases: "Sir Bors; Bors the Younger",
    history: `Sir Bors is one of the greatest Knights of the Round Table and the cousin of Sir Lancelot. Unlike many Arthurian heroes, Bors is remembered not only for his skill in battle but also for his unwavering faith and moral character. He is one of the few knights considered pure enough to participate in the successful quest for the Holy Grail.

During the Grail Quest, Sir Bors faced numerous trials that tested his loyalty, compassion, and devotion to God. While many famous knights failed because of pride or personal weakness, Bors remained steadfast and faithful. Alongside Sir Galahad and Sir Percival, he became one of only three knights to witness the Holy Grail's miracles.

After Galahad's death and Percival's passing, Bors returned to Camelot as the sole survivor of the successful Grail Quest. He continued serving King Arthur until the kingdom's final days. Sir Bors is remembered as the ideal balance between knightly valor and spiritual devotion, representing faith, humility, and unwavering honor.`
