    period: "Arthurian Era",
    aliases: "Sir Tristan; Tristram",
    history: `Sir Tristan is one of the greatest knights of Arthurian legend and one of medieval literature's most famous tragic heroes. Although his story existed independently before being woven into the Arthurian cycle, later romances made him one of the finest Knights of the Round Table. He is celebrated as an unmatched swordsman, hunter, and musician.

Tristan is best known for his tragic romance with the Irish princess Isolde. Through fate—or in many versions, by accidentally drinking a magical love potion—the two fell deeply in love despite Isolde being promised to King Mark of Cornwall. Their forbidden relationship led to years of adventure, betrayal, exile, and heartbreak.

Despite his personal struggles, Tristan remained one of Camelot's greatest warriors and was often ranked alongside Lancelot as one of the strongest knights of the Round Table. His story ends tragically with the deaths of both Tristan and Isolde, making them enduring symbols of doomed love, courage, and sacrifice.`
